async #getHighestCount(): Promise<number> {
    const result = await this.#emailModel.findPage({
        filter: `created_at:<=${new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0]}`,
        order: 'csd_email_count DESC',
        limit: 1
    });

// For high volume senders (400k+), cap the increase at 20% or 75k absolute
if (lastCount >= WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.threshold) {
    const scaledIncrease = Math.ceil(lastCount * WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.maxScale);
    const absoluteIncrease = lastCount + WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.maxAbsoluteIncrease;
    return Math.min(scaledIncrease, absoluteIncrease);
}

for (const threshold of WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.thresholds.sort((a, b) => a.limit - b.limit)) {
    if (lastCount < threshold.limit) {
        return Math.ceil(lastCount * threshold.scale);
    }
}

findings.body:
The `#getHighestCount` filter changed from `created_at:<${today}` to `created_at:<=${today}`. Nothing in the PR explains why. The JSDoc just above (line 98) says the count is for emails sent "excluding today", and the `<` form expresses that directly. Switching to `<=` makes the intent ambiguous and is not what the docs describe.

The existing unit test `should query for emails created before today` asserts `callArgs.filter.includes(`created_at:<${today}`)`. Because `created_at:<=2026-11-19` does not contain the substring `created_at:<2026-11-19`, that assertion now fails. The test was not updated, so either the change is accidental and should be reverted, or the test and docs need to be updated to match a deliberate decision.

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="101-105"}

The 'increases custom domain limit on subsequent day' test asserts `Math.min(email2Count, Math.ceil(csdCount1 * 1.25))`. Here csdCount1 comes from 100 members on day 1, which is at or below the base limit of 100, so the service's first-day result is 200 (capped by the total). On day 2 the service's lastCount is that same small value, so it again returns the base 200 and the outcome is `min(total, 200)`.

The 1.25 multiplier never affects the result: `ceil(100 * 1.25) = 125` and the service's `min(100, 200) = 100` both equal the email total, so any multiplier of 1.25 or more would pass. The test name and message promise a 1.25x increase but do not check one. Use a sender whose day-1 count is above 100 (for example, enough members to make csdCount1 around 200 and day 2 larger than 250) so the multiplier is actually observed.

::code{file="ghost/core/test/integration/services/email-service/domain-warming.test.js" lines="191-196"}

The final `return Math.ceil(lastCount * WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.maxScale)` runs only if the threshold loop finds no tier and lastCount is below 400k. The last threshold is `limit: 400_000`, so for any lastCount under 400k the loop returns, and any lastCount at or above 400k is handled by the high-volume branch. The fallback is dead code.

It is also misleading: it reuses `highVolume.maxScale` (1.2x) as if it were a general default, and its comment says "should not be reached". If that assumption ever breaks (for example someone edits the table so the last threshold is lower), the fallback silently applies a 1.2x growth rate with no cap rather than failing loudly. Either throw an error for an impossible state, or restructure the logic so the high-volume branch is the final tier and no fallback is needed.

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="137-138"}

The comment above the high-volume branch reads "cap the increase at 20% or 75k absolute". That wording could be read as an either-or, or as "the larger of the two". The code actually returns `Math.min(scaledIncrease, absoluteIncrease)`, which is the smaller of the two targets, so the effective rule is the smaller of +20% and +75k. The table in the JSDoc says `min(1.2x, +75k) cap`, which is correct, so the inline comment should match it.

::suggestion{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="124-124"}
// For high volume senders (400k+), limit growth to the smaller of +20% or +75k per send

`#getTargetLimit` calls `WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.thresholds.sort(...)` on each invocation. `Array.prototype.sort` sorts in place, so every call re-sorts the module-level config. This was already the case before the PR, and this PR makes the table the source of truth. It doesn't affect correctness today, because the array is already in ascending order. The sort is still a hidden side effect on a shared constant, and it would silently change behavior if the table order were ever edited and the sort removed.

Sorting once when the constant is defined, or documenting that the table must be ascending, would make the config easier to reason about.

::code{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="131-131"}
